This PR adds support for multi-color LEDs built with several monochromatic LED.

It imports the [leds-group-multicolor DT binding from Linux](https://www.kernel.org/doc/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/leds/leds-group-multicolor.yaml) and introduces the leds-group-multicolor driver.

Here is an example of DT fragment that defines an RGB LED with a group of monochromatic PWM LEDs:
```
/ { 
        monochromatic-leds { 
                compatible = "pwm-leds";

                red_pwm_led: led-0 { 
                        pwms = <&pwm1 1 PWM_MSEC(20) PWM_POLARITY_NORMAL>;
                };

                green_pwm_led: led-1 { 
                        pwms = <&pwm2 1 PWM_MSEC(20) PWM_POLARITY_NORMAL>;
                };

                blue_pwm_led: led-2 { 
                        pwms = <&pwm3 1 PWM_MSEC(20) PWM_POLARITY_NORMAL>;
                };
        };

        multi-led { 
                compatible = "leds-group-multicolor";

                leds = <&red_pwm_led>, <&green_pwm_led>, <&blue_pwm_led>;
                label = "RGB LED";
                color-mapping = <LED_COLOR_ID_RED>,
                                <LED_COLOR_ID_GREEN>,
                                <LED_COLOR_ID_BLUE>;
        };
};
```
The leds-group-multicolor driver implements the `->set_color()` API method. Instead of calling `led_set_brightness()` for each monochromatic LED, `led_set_color()` can be called on the `leds-group-multicolor` compatible device to configure all the LEDs at once.

Note that the `color-mapping` DT property, shown in the above example, is not mandatory.

Allowing to "freely" specify the SPI WORD SIZE allows to use this driver implementation with a nucleo_u5a5zj_q and other stm32 boards as stm32 does not support WORD SIZE other than 8/16 (see #95035)

in `drivers/spi/spi_ll_stm32.c`, we have

```
if ((SPI_WORD_SIZE_GET(config->operation) != 8)
	  && (SPI_WORD_SIZE_GET(config->operation) != 16)) {
return -ENOTSUP;
}
```

### Problem description

Manual resource cleanup is error-prone in C. When you acquire a resource (lock, memory, file handle), you must remember to release it on every exit path - normal returns, early returns, error conditions, etc. Missing even one cleanup leads to resource leaks or deadlocks.

### Proposed Change

This PR introduces scope-based cleanup helpers similar to the [Linux kernel Scope-based cleanup helpers](https://docs.kernel.org/core-api/cleanup.html). A RAII-like pattern for C - Brings the safety of C++ RAII, Go's defer, or Java's try-with-resources to C.

The Zephyr project can provide these helper constructs for users to write safer **_application code_**, if supported by the compiler.

[doc preview](https://builds.zephyrproject.io/zephyr/pr/101264/docs/kernel/cleanup.html)

See the following sample of how a mutex lock guard simplifies the code:
```c
/* Before: Manual unlock needed */
static int foo(void)
{
	int ret = 0;

	k_mutex_lock(&lock, K_FOREVER);
	/* code body without return statements and goto jumps */

exit_label:
	k_mutex_unlock(&lock);
	return ret;
}
```
```c
/* After: Automatic unlock when scope ends */
static int bar(void)
{
	scope_guard(k_mutex)(&lock);
	/* code body that allows early returns */

	return 0;
}
/* Alternative: Defer an unlock */
static int bar_alt(void)
{
	int ret = k_mutex_lock(&lock, K_NO_WAIT);
	if (ret < 0) {
		return ret;
	}
	scope_defer(k_mutex_unlock)(&lock);
	/* code body that allows early returns */

	return 0;
}
```

Automatic cleanup of allocated resources using `DEFER(k_free)`:

```c
static void baz(void)
{
	void *ptr = k_malloc(42);
	scope_defer(k_free)(ptr); // <- safe even if ptr is NULL

	/* code body */
}
```

### Concerns and Unresolved Questions

- Compiler dependent, so this can only be used in application code, not in the kernel or subsystems!
- Macro names (uppercase vs lowercase `guard`/`defer`)
- Put it behind a Kconfig option?

### Alternatives Considered

There's no alternative AFAIK for calling a cleanup function at the end of a scope. The closest alternative would be some macro abusing a `for`-loop scope with a custom break statement (no returns!).

### Problem Description

Zephyr currently lacks native driver support for hard real-time motor control applications. Existing APIs have critical limitations that prevent their use in FOC implementations:

- **PWM Synchronization:** The `pwm_set_dt()` API updates channels sequentially rather than atomically. This delay causes phase shifts and induces torque ripple in 3-phase motor systems.

- **ADC Determinism:** Reliance on software-triggered ADC results in sampling jitter and prevents precise synchronization with the PWM noise-free window (center-aligned sampling).

- **Fault Latency:** The software-based protection path (GPIO ISR, Comparator ISR) introduces latency exceeding the critical Short Circuit Withstand Time (SCWT) of power switches, risking hardware damage.

- **Inter-Peripheral Signaling:** The ecosystem lacks a standard abstraction for hardware event linking (e.g., direct Timer-to-ADC triggering) without CPU intervention.

### Related Issues

- External FOC implementation: https://github.com/teslabs/spinner
- Power Electronics Roadmap: #38629

### Proposed Change (Summary)

Two architectural solutions are proposed:

1. **Modular Drivers:** Extend existing PWM/ADC APIs and add new discrete drivers (PWM 3-Phase, Fault). This approach aligns with Zephyr's architecture but requires software coordination.

2. **Unified Inverter Driver:** A single driver encapsulating PWM + ADC + Fault + hardware linking. This provides optimal real-time performance but is less reusable.

### Proposed Change (Detailed)

#### Solution 1: Modular Drivers

<img width="1064" height="351" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/32da7481-289a-44dd-bcea-ea304197da6a" />

Extend existing APIs and create new discrete drivers:

| Component | Approach |
|-----------|----------|
| ADC | Extend API with `adc_set_hardware_trigger()` |
| PWM 3-Phase | New driver with atomic update + deadtime |
| Fault | New driver for hardware protection path |

##### PWM 3-Phase Driver

New driver class for synchronized 3-phase PWM generation with complementary outputs and dead-time insertion.

**API:**

```c
int pwm_3ph_start(const struct device *dev);
int pwm_3ph_set_frequency(const struct device *dev, uint32_t freq_hz);
int pwm_3ph_set_deadtime(const struct device *dev, uint32_t rising_ns, uint32_t falling_ns);

int pwm_3ph_set_duty(const struct device *dev, uint16_t duty_u, uint16_t duty_v, uint16_t duty_w);
int pwm_3ph_set_callback(const struct device *dev, pwm_3ph_callback_t cb, void *user_data);
```

**Devicetree Binding:**

```dts
pwm_3ph0: pwm-3phase {
    compatible = "vnd,pwm-3phase";
    status = "okay";
    pwm-u = <&gpt0>;
    pwm-v = <&gpt1>;
    pwm-w = <&gpt2>;
    deadtime-rising-ns = <500>;
    deadtime-falling-ns = <500>;
};
```

##### ADC API Extension

Extend existing ADC API to support hardware-triggered sampling.

**API:**

```c
int adc_set_hardware_trigger(const struct device *dev,
                             uint32_t trigger_event_id);
int adc_clear_hardware_trigger(const struct device *dev);
```

##### Fault Driver

New driver class for hardware-based overcurrent and fault protection.

**API:**

```c
int fault_enable(const struct device *dev);
int fault_disable(const struct device *dev);
int fault_get_status(const struct device *dev, uint32_t *status);
int fault_clear(const struct device *dev);
int fault_set_callback(const struct device *dev, fault_callback_t cb, void *user_data);
```

**Devicetree Binding:**

```dts
fault0: fault {
    compatible = "renesas,fault-protection";
    status = "okay";
    poeg = <&poeg0>;
};
```

#### Solution 2: Unified Inverter Driver

<img width="1070" height="298" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/96f07ca3-7fe9-4cc5-8fa0-045a1fe63642" />

Single driver encapsulating PWM + ADC + Fault + hardware event linking. All inter-peripheral synchronization is handled internally.

**API:**

```c
int inverter_start(const struct device *dev);
int inverter_stop(const struct device *dev);
int inverter_set_frequency(const struct device *dev, uint32_t freq_hz);
int inverter_set_deadtime(const struct device *dev, uint32_t rising_ns, uint32_t falling_ns);
int inverter_set_duty(const struct device *dev, uint16_t duty_u, uint16_t duty_v, uint16_t duty_w);
int inverter_get_phase_current(const struct device *dev, int32_t *i_u, int32_t *i_v, int32_t *i_w);
int inverter_get_fault_status(const struct device *dev, uint32_t *status);
int inverter_clear_fault(const struct device *dev);
int inverter_set_fault_callback(const struct device *dev, inverter_fault_callback_t cb, void *user_data);
int inverter_set_control_callback(const struct device *dev, inverter_control_callback_t cb, void *user_data);
```

**Devicetree Binding:**

```dts
inverter0: inverter {
    compatible = "renesas,inverter-3phase";
    status = "okay";

    /* 3-Phase PWM Timer Instances */
    pwm-u = <&gpt0>;
    pwm-v = <&gpt1>;
    pwm-w = <&gpt2>;
    deadtime-ns = <1000>;

    /* Current Feedback via ADC */
    io-channels = <&adc0 0>, <&adc0 1>, <&adc0 2>;
    io-channel-names = "i_phase_u", "i_phase_v", "i_phase_w";

    /* Hardware Interconnects & Safety */
    elc = <&elc>;
    poeg = <&poeg0>;
};
```

### Concerns and Unresolved Questions

Should the architecture prioritize Zephyr API alignment (Solution 1) or real-time performance (Solution 2)?

Add support for CS IPT (Channel Sounding Inline PCT Transfer) in the Bluetooth host stack.

Changes include:
* definition of new CS IPT HCI opcodes;
* handling of CS IPT control procedures;
* integration with existing CS infrastructure;
* necessary updates to public headers.

## Summary

- Add QST Corporation to vendor prefixes
- Add driver for the QST QMI8658A 6-axis IMU (accelerometer + gyroscope) over I2C
- Configurable full-scale range, ODR, and data-ready interrupt pin (INT1 or INT2) via devicetree
- Trigger support via global thread or dedicated thread

Tested on Sipeed M0Sense (BL702) with accel, gyro, die temperature readout, and data-ready trigger on INT1.

## Supported features

- Accelerometer: channels X/Y/Z/XYZ, full-scale 2/4/8/16 g
- Gyroscope: channels X/Y/Z/XYZ, full-scale 16–2048 dps
- Die temperature
- ODR selection (28 Hz – 7174 Hz, 6-DoF effective rates)
- Data-ready interrupt on INT1 or INT2 (configurable via `int-pin` property)
- Trigger modes: none, global thread, own thread

## Example devicetree

```dts
&i2c0 {
    qmi8658a: qmi8658a@6a {
        compatible = "qst,qmi8658a";
        reg = <0x6a>;
        accel-fs = <4>;      /* 4 g */
        gyro-fs = <512>;     /* 512 dps */
        accel-odr = <896>;   /* ~896 Hz */
        gyro-odr = <896>;
        int-gpios = <&gpio0 0 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
        int-pin = <1>;
    };
};

# Summary
Add a gnss driver for the GlobalTop PA6H that uses the PMTK protocol. I tested it with the [Adafruit Ultimate GPS](https://learn.adafruit.com/adafruit-ultimate-gps) breakout board and the [gnss sample](https://github.com/zephyrproject-rtos/zephyr/blob/main/samples/drivers/gnss/src/main.c), which is based on this module.

Below is the test results with debug output. Lots of noise there but I checked that the fix rate was set, output message according to fix rate, get enabled system, and fix message is working as expected.

