According to errata ES0596, some STM32H7RS devices have factory calibration data for IO compensation stored in OTP memory. This commit adds support for reading these values and applying them to the IO compensation cells.

Usage example:
```
&iocell {
	status = "okay";

	nvmem-cells = <&iocomp_vddxspi1_1v8_otp>, <&iocomp_vddxspi2_1v8_otp>;
	nvmem-cell-names = "vddxspi1_comp", "vddxspi2_comp";

	vddxspi1 {
		hslv-mode = "on";
		compensation-enabled;
	};

	vddxspi2 {
		hslv-mode = "on";
		compensation-enabled;
	};
};
```

…de over SPI

Currently, the WiFi driver for the SiWx91x only works for projects running on the SiWx91x. This commit implements the SPI interface and assumes that the necessary files are included in the wiseconnect SDK, namely:

`wiseconnect/components/device/silabs/si91x/wireless/inc/sl_si91x_spi_constants.h` 
`wiseconnect/components/device/silabs/si91x/wireless/inc/sl_status.h`
`wiseconnect/components/device/silabs/si91x/wireless/ncp_interface/sl_si91x_ncp_driver.c`
`wiseconnect/components/device/silabs/si91x/wireless/ncp_interface/spi/sl_si91x_spi.c`

Only `CONFIG_WIFI_SILABS_SIWX91X_NET_STACK_NATIVE` has been tested successfully on an `stm32f469i_disco` board connected to a Si-EB8045A adapter board with the Co-processor radio board BRD4357A.

Add the device to the spi bus like:

`&spix {
	siwn917: siwx91x@0 {
        status = "okay";
		compatible = "silabs,siwx91x-nwp-spi";
		siwx91x_wifi: wifi {
			compatible = "silabs,siwx91x-wifi";
		};
	};
};`

BLE should also be supported in this configuration; however, it has not been demonstrated to work yet. Due to the structure of the zephyr project and how the SiWx91x is initialized, there is BLE configuration code in the `wifi/siwx91x` folder which should obviously be changed eventually.

Signed-off-by: Ethan Sisk <ethan@firehud.co>
Assisted-by: GitHub Copilot:claude-opus-4.6

### Overview

This RFC introduces remote-side support for RemoteProc based on the OpenAMP library.
The implementation converts the existing sample
(samples/subsys/ipc/openamp_rsc_table/src/main_remote.c) into a reusable driver and extends it to support devicetree-based configuration.

### Features
- RemoteProc driver for the remote processor using OpenAMP
- Devicetree-based description of:
   - resource tables
   - carveouts (shared memory regions)
   - VirtIO devices and vrings
- Automatic generation of the OpenAMP resource table from devicetree
- Support for carveouts, enabling more complex memory mappings
- RPMsg VirtIO device driver
- RPMsg logging backend with fallback to the RAM console

### Motivation
This work enables a flexible and reusable way to describe OpenAMP resources in devicetree instead of hardcoding them in application code.

Carveout support is particularly important for systems with complex memory translation setups (e.g. MMU/IOMMU).

### Use Case
The implementation is used for RemoteProc over PCIe on a
AMD Zynq-7000 SoC (Cortex-A9) connected to a Linux host.

### Test Setup

- Hardware: Duebon TEC0097 PCIe evaluation board
- FPGA design: https://github.com/mausys/tec0097-pcie
- FSBL (with bitstream): https://github.com/mausys/zynq-fsbl
- Linux kernel module (with RemoteProc over PCIe support): https://github.com/mausys/rproc_pcie
- Zephyr application: https://github.com/mausys/zephyr-zynq

### Devicetree Example

Below is a simplified example showing how resources are described:
```
vdev0vring0: memory@1400 {
		compatible= "zephyr,memory-region";
		zephyr,memory-region = "vdev0vring0";
		reg = <0x1400 0x2000>;
	};

	vdev0vring1: memory@3400 {
		compatible= "zephyr,memory-region";
		zephyr,memory-region = "vdev0vring1";
		reg = <0x3400 0x2000>;
	};

	vdev0buffer: memory@5400 {
		compatible= "zephyr,memory-region";
		zephyr,memory-region = "vdev0buffer";
		reg = <0x5400 0x8000>;
	};

	remoteproc {
		rsc_tbl: resource_table {
			compatible = "openamp,resource-table";
			carveouts = <&vdev0vring0 &vdev0vring1 &vdev0buffer>;
			vdevs = <&vdev0>;
		};

		vrings {
			#address-cells = <1>;
			#size-cells = <0>;

			vdev0_tx: vring@0 {
				compatible = "openamp,vring";
				#vring-cells = <0>;
				notify-id = <0>;
				memory-region = <&vdev0vring0>;
				num-buffers = <8>;
				mboxes = <&mbox 0>;
				reg = <0>;
			};

			vdev0_rx: vring@1 {
				compatible = "openamp,vring";
				#vring-cells = <0>;
				notify-id = <1>;
				memory-region = <&vdev0vring1>;
				num-buffers = <8>;
				mboxes = <&mbox 0>;
				reg = <1>;
			};
		};

		vdevs {
			#address-cells = <1>;
			#size-cells = <0>;

			vdev0: vdev@0 {
				compatible = "openamp,rpmsg-virtio-device";
				vrings = <&vdev0_tx &vdev0_rx>;
				vring-names = "tx", "rx";
				memory-region = <&vdev0buffer>;
				reg = <0>;
			};
		};
	};
```
```
&vdev0 {
	#address-cells = <1>;
	#size-cells = <0>;

	ept_log: endpoint@0 {
		status = "okay";
		compatible = "openamp,rpmsg-endpoint";
		type = "rpmsg-raw";
		reg = <0x0>;
	};
  
  
	ept_rpc: endpoint@11 {
		status = "okay";
		compatible = "openamp,rpmsg-endpoint";
		type = "rpmsg-raw";
		reg = <0x11>;
	};
};
```

### Notes / RFC Points

Feedback on devicetree bindings and naming is highly appreciated
API naming (z_rproc_*) may need adjustment if promoted to public interfaces

When using bigger messages, some issues appeared:
  - MCTP over UART uses asynchronous UART, but when libmctp breaks a big message in smaller packets, it tries to send them sequentially: if the previous one didn't complete, the following would silently fail. One can argue that libmctp behaviour here is not ideal, but a fix there is another task. For now, use a semaphore to serialise UART TX;
  - MCTP over UART and I2C+GPIO  also had some issues for the tests (there's no proper "teardown phase" when using libmctp), so some new facilities to handle test specific constraints were added;
  - Fix a possible race on MCTP over I3C;
  - Fix the I2C+GPIO sample that was replying MCTP message from ISR context.

Some device driver APIs embed and extend other ones (e.g. I3C extends I2C). Introduce `DEVICE_API_EXTENDS` to declare parent-child relationships between device API classes, and update `DEVICE_API_IS` to use a new `_ext_end` linker symbol as the upper bound of its range check.

All device API entries are placed in a single output section (`device_api_area`). Child class entries are placed after their parent's entries using a depth-first walk, and an `_ext_end` symbol is emitted after all descendants. This makes `DEVICE_API_IS(parent_class, dev)` return true for devices with a child API, and naturally supports multi-level inheritance.

Example linker output for parent i2c with child i3c:

```ld
SECTION_PROLOGUE(device_api_area,,SUBALIGN(4))
...
	Z_LINK_ITERABLE(i2c_driver_api);
	Z_LINK_ITERABLE(i3c_driver_api);
	_i3c_driver_api_ext_end = .;
	_i2c_driver_api_ext_end = .;
...
}
```

