## Summary

Fix PCIe Virtual Channel register access and state updates.

Zephyr's PCIe configuration helpers use DWORD indices, and `pcie_get_ext_cap()` returns the extended capability base in those units. The VC code previously added byte offsets directly to that base, so it accessed the wrong configuration registers.

This change:

- converts VC register offsets to the DWORD units used by the PCIe configuration API;
- writes Resource Control updates back to configuration space;
- uses explicit masks and shifts instead of compiler-dependent register bitfields;
- validates internal register-helper pointers;
- treats `vc_count` as the total number of VCs, including VC0;
- waits for any pre-existing Negotiation Pending condition to clear before changing VC Enable/Disable;
- waits again for the negotiation triggered by the control write;
- bounds both waits so enable/disable cannot hang indefinitely.

## Tests

The simulated PCI configuration-space ztests cover:

- exact register addressing and write-back;
- VC enable/disable;
- traffic-class mapping and VC-count semantics;
- a pre-existing pending negotiation that clears before the write;
- a pre-existing pending negotiation that times out with zero writes;
- a post-write negotiation that clears;
- a post-write negotiation that times out;
- missing capability and invalid-map paths.

